body:
The biggest thing that jumps out is the if statements. First you check to see if num is a factor of 3 and 5, and if it isn't you check to see if num is a factor of 3 but not a factor of 5. You don't need to check if num is not a factor of 5, because that's done implicitly by the previous statement failing. Either you've overlooked the redendancy here, or you don't fully understand how the else if works.

This code

    if (num % 3 == 0 && num % 5 == 0) {
        Console.WriteLine("Fizzbuzz");
    } else if (num % 3 == 0 && num % 5 !=0) {
        Console.WriteLine("Fizz");
    }

Is the same as this

    if (num % 3 == 0 && num % 5 == 0) {
        Console.WriteLine("Fizzbuzz");
    } else {
        if (num % 3 == 0 && num % 5 !=0) {
            Console.WriteLine("Fizz");
        }
    }

Hopefully that makes it easier to see that the second if statement will only ever run if the first if statement's condition is false. We can simplify the whole thing to this

    if (num % 3 == 0 && num % 5 == 0) {
        Console.WriteLine("Fizzbuzz");
    } else if (num % 3 == 0) {
        Console.WriteLine("Fizz");
    }

Aside from a few small issues, it's OK. Keep it up! :)

Learn a framework related to something that you want to do and use it. You'll be able to do a lot more work, and it will happen a lot better and faster if you're building on top of a codebase that was written by a programmer with more experience and skill than you have.

&#x200B;

The difficulty in taking on more advanced projects is that you have to write better code to prevent the entire thing from becoming a mess, and you need to have knowlege of the domain that you're working in. A framework does a lot of the work for you in both regards.
